#Git and GitHub  Workflow Steps 

Step 1
git log/status - checks whether the current dir is a git repo or not (assuming it's not).

Step 2
git init - initializes the dir into a git repo indicated by the .git extension at the end of the path.

Step 3
git status - if the dir (now a git repo) had files before its git init, they will appear in red letters as untracked (step 6 deals with this in detail, at
this point the intention is to confirm that this is now a git repo).

Step 4
git remote - checks for remote version control server connections (e.g., GitHub, the popular option).
No connection will be detected because GitHub connects to Git repos, not regular directories. This will output
nothing to the console.

Step 4
git remote add origin <GitHub Link to Repo here> - This will establish a connection  between the local repo
and the GitHub remote origin repo (origin is the industry-standard convention alias for the default remote repo name).

Step 5
git remote - This command confirms whether a remote repo is connected to the local or not; in this case, it is (see step 4)
and the output will be "origin" (see step 4 on the alias origin).

Step 6
git status - checks the working tree for any files. Assuming there were files in the dir before step 2, those files
will appear in red as untracked. It is important to make at least one commit before establishing a Git and GitHub repos
connection. If a connection is established without the implementation of a single commit, a message of divergent histories
will be logged on the console. If this is the case, a better step is to run the following CLI command: rm -rf <.git>
The command tells git to recursively (r - apply the command actions to the git repo and all its subdirectories) forcefully (f -
overrides any caution for sensitive metadata, for example, config files and so on). The exclusion of the -f flag and only running the -r flag will
require manual confirmation for the deletion of every sensitive metadata, which can be time-consuming. So only use the -rf flag when
sure that deletion of the .git repo data is intended.

Step 6
git add - This will stage and track the files (previously marked red and untracked), confirmed by the color change to green.
This indicates that the files are now ready to be committed. If you have more than one file and want to commit them all at once, add a period at
the end of the command like so: (git add .), but if not and you prefer committing individually, use (git add <file name here>).


Step 7
git commit -m <"Descriptive commit message here in parenthesis"> - This will establish your first commit.

Step 8
git status - At this stage, there should be no untracked or unstaged files in the working tree.

Step 9
git pull origin main - It is important to first PULL before you PUSH to avoid the divergent histories notification message.
This command tells Git to pull (copy) the version history in the remote origin repo from the branch main to the local repo
on your local machine.

Step 10
git log - This command logs the entire commit history to your local repo (step 9).

Step 11
git push -u origin main - This command tells Git to establish a connection between the local and remote repos. The -u flag (upstream) is the linking
modifier that connects the local repo to the remote origin repo's branch main. From now on, when you push (always pulling before pushing), the argumement/pathspac
(origin main) is not necessary.
    
